WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.110
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:07.755
MICHEAL KELLY:
Wanted to start out--

00:00:07.755 --> 00:00:10.380
kick it off by talking a little
bit about the state of database

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.870
monitoring today.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:15.840
We'll spend a couple of minutes
on this talk about Stackdriver

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:19.075
and BindPlane and
then go into a demo

00:00:19.075 --> 00:00:21.450
and finally talk with one of
the early customers that are

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.690
actually using this solution.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.190
But the reason to talk about
the state of database mattering

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.650
is just to get on the same page.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.910
Can I ask how many
people here-- is

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:36.360
it your primary responsibility
to be managing databases inside

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.033
of your environment?

00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:39.450
And then for how
many people is it

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:45.240
a secondary or tertiary
responsibility for you?

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.250
So I would imagine a
lot of you have already

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.620
been through this experience,
but in the last five to 10

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.400
years, there've been a couple
of pretty significant changes

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.730
in the database landscape.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:59.220
If we go back that period
of time, a lot of companies

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.328
were really just focused on
maybe one proprietary database,

00:01:03.328 --> 00:01:05.370
and they would use that
in almost every situation

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:06.910
inside of their environment.

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:07.865
And they were run it
on the same thing.

00:01:07.865 --> 00:01:09.448
So it'll be-- a lot
of times you would

00:01:09.448 --> 00:01:12.870
have a proprietary database that
you're paying licensing fees.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.455
Maybe it was running
on prem on VMware.

00:01:15.455 --> 00:01:16.830
And that was
basically everything

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.740
that you were dealing with in
it was much simpler than what

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.140
we're seeing today
because really what we've

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.180
seen over the last few years
is a pretty quick migration

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.260
not only to the cloud--

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.690
[INAUDIBLE] dealing
with that same database.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:34.150
It maybe migrated to the cloud,
or it may be in both locations

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.180
but then also in the
number of database types

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.540
that we're dealing with.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.915
And that's really where we've
seen the biggest explosion.

00:01:39.915 --> 00:01:42.600
So it started with
companies moving

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.850
to open source
databases, the same types

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:46.317
of relational
databases, but they

00:01:46.317 --> 00:01:48.150
didn't want to pay those
same fees that they

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.400
were paying in the past.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.680
But they still they
still kept those prior--

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.180
the databases they
had previously.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.480
So they still had the
Oracles and the SQL servers,

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:00.990
but they just added
in another layer.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:04.680
Then really the next step was
to move to NoSQL databases so

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.690
a lot of cluster databases.

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:10.410
And then finally we'd moved
to fully hosted in the cloud

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:11.730
databases.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.230
So really what
that says is we've

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:17.370
got this hugely complex
problem that we didn't have

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:20.700
to deal with a few years
ago, and what we've noticed

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:22.860
is the tools really
haven't kept up.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.905
So you don't have an
easy solution out there,

00:02:24.905 --> 00:02:26.280
and that's the
problem that we're

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.640
trying to solve here with
BindPlane and with Blue Medora

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.071
with BindPlane and Stackdriver.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:37.900
So if I step back
and say let's start

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.550
by trying to find a
common set of things

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:42.550
that we're looking for
in all of these databases

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:45.520
because we know we have--
maybe we have half a dozen

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.800
to a dozen inside
of our environments

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:48.800
that we need to monitor.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.810
We want to try and find the
common threads between those.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:56.470
And really what we're looking
for in all situations is--

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:57.970
in roughly in order,
we want to know

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:00.700
the health and availability
of the databases.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.725
We want to understand
the performance--

00:03:03.725 --> 00:03:06.100
we need to be able to see the
performance of the database

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:09.700
and understand when there's
a clear degradation.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:12.330
We want alerts
from the databases,

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:14.830
things like root cause analysis
so the ability to figure out

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.360
why this is happening.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.040
And then finally optimization
so the ability to see capacity--

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:24.850
to plan capacity and we
want that across the board.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:27.160
So that's number one what
we're looking for here.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.020
But it's a really good
idea not to stop there.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:35.500
What we really want to do
is go one level further

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:38.315
and look at the
infrastructure underneath it.

00:03:38.315 --> 00:03:39.940
And this is more
challenging because we

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:42.580
have a situation now
where you have some that

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:46.660
are in the cloud, some that are
on prem, some may be in GCP,

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:50.170
but you may also be using
other public clouds as well.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:52.415
And that can make these
types of metrics challenging,

00:03:52.415 --> 00:03:54.040
but if you really
want to get to a spot

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:57.310
where you're not
pointing fingers

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:58.750
throughout the
organization, it's

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:00.042
important to have all of these.

00:04:03.192 --> 00:04:05.150
So this is the challenge
that we're faced with.

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:08.450
If we want to be able
to get all those metrics

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:11.105
and get that visibility at the
database and infrastructure

00:04:11.105 --> 00:04:14.150
layer, we have a
lot of data sources

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:17.089
that we're going to--
that we need to deal with.

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:19.970
From the metric side,
we have API metrics

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:22.815
for a lot of your
NoSQL databases.

00:04:22.815 --> 00:04:25.190
For Cassandra, you're going
to have to go through an API.

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:28.460
You'll have JDBC for all
the traditional relational

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:31.610
databases, but it's going to
be different for each one.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.970
We have logs for queries and
alerts for both of those.

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.530
And then the same thing done
at the infrastructure layer,

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.530
we need to get the cloud
metrics, virtualization

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:43.740
metrics, host logs
for both of those.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.130
So getting all of that and
pulling it all together

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:49.390
is one of the big
challenges here.

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:51.210
So let's talk about
the solutions.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.752
MARIE COSGROVE-DAVIES:
So Mike's just

00:04:52.752 --> 00:04:54.780
described a sticky
situation that a lot of us

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:56.790
found ourselves in recently.

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:57.700
What can we use?

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:59.700
What tools are available
to help us mitigate

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.770
this to give us a
single pane of glass

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:04.030
set of observability tools?

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:06.180
The first I'm going to
talk about is Stackdriver.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:09.840
How many of you are familiar
with or use Stackdriver?

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:11.860
So I'm not going to spend
too much time on this,

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:13.950
but I want to remind you
just in case you only

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:15.533
use part of Stackdriver
and you're not

00:05:15.533 --> 00:05:17.490
aware of all of
the other features,

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:20.280
Stackdriver is Google Cloud's
monitoring and observability

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:20.940
tool.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:23.580
We also have application
performance management tools

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:24.510
as well.

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:27.240
Stackdriver has
three basic pillars.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.360
There's monitoring, which
will be our focus today,

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:34.320
there's logging, and there's
APM as I just mentioned.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.880
And for all Google
Cloud Services,

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:38.040
this all happens automagically.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:41.172
You spin up your GCP
instance of whatever it is,

00:05:41.172 --> 00:05:43.380
and you get your metrics
and your logs in Stackdriver

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.113
without any additional
instrumentation.

00:05:45.113 --> 00:05:46.530
So what we're
really talking about

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:49.050
is adding that level
of observability

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.300
for your third-party
applications

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:52.950
that you're running
on Google Cloud

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:54.783
or your third-party
applications that you're

00:05:54.783 --> 00:05:57.090
running on prem or in
other clouds as well

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:59.070
as the underlying
services that you're

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:02.468
using on prem in other clouds.

00:06:02.468 --> 00:06:04.010
And what solution
are we using to get

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:06.680
those metrics and those
logs into Stackdriver?

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:08.420
We're using Blue
Medora's BindPlane.

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:11.570
BindPlane is essentially an
agent as a service offering

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:13.340
that allows you for
metrics to deploy

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:15.680
a single metric collector
to your environment.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:18.290
As long as it can talk to all
of your different services

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:20.972
that you're monitoring, it can
pull those metrics together,

00:06:20.972 --> 00:06:22.430
send them off to
BindPlane and then

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:23.722
send them along to Stackdriver.

00:06:23.722 --> 00:06:25.555
I'm not going to spend
too much time talking

00:06:25.555 --> 00:06:27.847
through this picture because
you'll see our demo later,

00:06:27.847 --> 00:06:29.990
and that's a little more
interactive and exciting.

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:31.990
But I do want to mention
that BindPlane supports

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:35.240
about 150 different services
ranging from bare metal

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:38.330
hardware all the way up
to third-party application

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:39.650
services.

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:41.570
And it's available
at no additional cost

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:43.070
to Stackdriver users.

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:45.290
So if you're sending your
metrics into Stackdriver,

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:47.330
you'll pay Stackdriver
for your custom metrics,

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:49.247
but you're not going to
pay BindPlane as well.

00:06:49.247 --> 00:06:52.170
There's no added fee.

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:54.980
We're talking specifically
about databases here,

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:56.730
and we did want to
list out the databases.

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:57.810
This is a list today.

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:00.510
There are several others
that are in the pipe,

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:04.230
but BindPlane offers about
12 different integrations.

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:05.730
If there is a
database that you care

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:08.410
about that isn't on this list,
come up and talk to us after.

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:11.082
Come find Blue Medora at
their booth or come find me.

00:07:11.082 --> 00:07:12.540
We would love to
get your feedback,

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.970
and there's a pretty
fast turnaround time

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:17.640
on all of the
instrumentation that's

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:19.775
required to add a new service.

00:07:19.775 --> 00:07:21.900
We're also going to talk
about these specific types

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:25.080
of infrastructure so Google
Cloud obviously but also

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:27.840
on premise to have
virtualized and bare metal.

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:33.067
And we'll touch on other cloud
vendors' observability as well.

00:07:33.067 --> 00:07:35.400
Again, I'm not going to go
too much into depth with this

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:37.790
because the demo is a
little more interesting,

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:40.170
but BindPlane gives us
a huge amount of control

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:43.567
over those metrics that we're
pulling from a given service.

00:07:43.567 --> 00:07:45.150
You may not be
interested in anything,

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:46.800
and BindPlane
gives you KPIs that

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:49.530
are flagged as things that you
probably do want to report on.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:53.040
But this is just a view of
the level of configurability

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:54.930
where you can select
exactly which metrics

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:56.610
are important to you
and make sure those

00:07:56.610 --> 00:07:58.470
are available in Stackdriver.

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:04.377
And then you can see here on
the right what observability

00:08:04.377 --> 00:08:05.460
looks like in Stackdriver.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:08.370
We've got here a slow
query log showing up

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:11.162
in Stackdriver monitoring.

00:08:11.162 --> 00:08:13.620
And so this is the solution
that we're talking about today,

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:17.820
centralizing database monitoring
across clouds, across on prem

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.960
with Stackdriver and BindPlane.

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:21.400
We are focusing on monitoring.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:24.150
I do want to mention that
logging support is also

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:26.550
in alpha right now
with BindPlane,

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:28.860
and so if you're interested
in joining that program,

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:30.450
please come talk to us after.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:33.360
That really allows you to
centralize all of your signals

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.730
for your databases in one place.

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:39.330
Demo time.

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:40.205
MICHEAL KELLY: Great.

00:08:40.205 --> 00:08:41.370
Thanks, Marie.

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:42.918
So we're going to
go into a demo.

00:08:42.918 --> 00:08:44.710
Going to talk about a
few different things.

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:48.717
One, how do you set up
BindPlane and Stackdriver?

00:08:48.717 --> 00:08:50.550
We'll do a quick overview
of the Stackdriver

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:53.670
in the database
monitoring solution

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:55.800
and then go through
how you would

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:59.427
solve a couple of problems
using this combination.

00:08:59.427 --> 00:09:02.010
And then at the end, I'm going
to take a few minutes just take

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:05.325
a look at our new logs alpha
that we have available.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:15.410
So what we're looking
at here is BindPlane.

00:09:15.410 --> 00:09:16.870
It's a product by Blue Medora.

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:19.510
It's a SaaS application.

00:09:19.510 --> 00:09:21.790
And the intent of
it is like we've

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:26.770
mentioned to extend Stackdriver
beyond just Google products

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:31.210
into databases but
also infrastructure,

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:35.440
other clouds, other workloads
that you may be interested in.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.480
And the way it
works is we have--

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:40.920
in this case, I'm monitoring
three different data centers.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:42.700
So the one data
center on the right

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:48.130
is on prem data center
for monitoring VMware.

00:09:48.130 --> 00:09:50.620
We're monitoring a number of
different physical systems

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:56.590
like Pure Storage, some Cisco
networking gear as well as

00:09:56.590 --> 00:10:00.220
servers, and then a range
of different database types

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:01.460
as well.

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:02.925
So we're gathering
all that data.

00:10:02.925 --> 00:10:04.300
We're sending it
up to BindPlane,

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:07.810
and then it's going
directly to Stackdriver.

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:11.585
We also have a data center
here that is GCP based.

00:10:11.585 --> 00:10:13.960
And in this case, you'll notice
we're not monitoring GCP.

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:16.030
That as we mentioned
is already baked in,

00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:19.150
so you have availability--
you have the observability

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:22.010
into all the GCP metrics
for the infrastructure.

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:23.980
But we do add
monitoring for things

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:26.830
like databases or
any other workloads

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:29.170
that you could be running there.

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:32.470
And then the last data center is
another public cloud that we're

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:35.260
monitoring where
we can pull data

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:39.310
from those specific services
that you're using there

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:44.510
as well as databases or other
workloads you could be running.

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:46.698
The idea is that
you can use this

00:10:46.698 --> 00:10:48.490
even if you are running
on multiple clouds,

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:53.490
even if you have a data center,
or have databases on prem.

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:57.100
We can pull it all together
into a single solution

00:10:57.100 --> 00:11:02.560
and then get visibility to all
that inside of Stackdriver.

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:05.785
So let's take a closer look
at the collector first.

00:11:09.350 --> 00:11:12.710
So I mentioned that we
have a collector installed

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:13.840
in each data center.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:16.400
So this doesn't need
to be installed locally

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:17.690
on your databases.

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:19.640
It will connect remotely
to those databases

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:24.380
as long as there's a
network visibility.

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:28.590
And we've made this as simple
as possible to install.

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:33.230
So you just choose the platform
that you want to install it on.

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:37.300
In this case, I'm just going
to choose Mac OS for a demo.

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:40.520
We have in most cases a single
line installer, so we just

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:47.615
copy this, paste
it in my terminal,

00:11:47.615 --> 00:11:49.740
and depending on the network
connection speed here,

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:52.710
it's going to download
the collector.

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:59.990
And really this is a very
small, small collector

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:01.740
because it doesn't
have any of the plugin.

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:05.190
So the 150 or so different
extensions that we support

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:07.570
aren't downloaded
all at the same time.

00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:09.460
What the collector does is--

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:12.960
it's a simple
Java-based application

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:15.630
that can run any
of these plugins

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:17.040
that I'm going to show you.

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:19.830
Can gather data and
is fully managed

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:20.960
by the BindPlane service.

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:22.710
So it's one of the
great things about this

00:12:22.710 --> 00:12:24.720
is you are unlikely
to need to go back

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:29.880
into the server to configure a
JSON file or modify anything.

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:33.040
Here we can handle that all
remotely through BindPlane

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:33.540
itself.

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:39.840
So if I flip back, I see that
the collector that I installed

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:42.720
locally has reached
back out to BindPlane,

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:45.160
and it's registered itself.

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:47.760
Now if I wanted to, I can
convert this to a group.

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:50.160
And this is, if you have
very large environments

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:53.610
or you have availability
requirements,

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:55.860
what we recommend
is building out

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:58.260
a cluster of these collectors.

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:00.630
It's very simple to do,
and it'll automatically

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:04.260
handle failover as well as
increasing the throughput

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:08.350
if you have a very
large environment.

00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:11.460
But for now, I'm just going to
leave that single collector.

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:13.470
We'll see that
it's now available

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:15.600
inside a BindPlane
as my new data

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:17.265
center, which is just my laptop.

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.285
And we need to add a source.

00:13:22.285 --> 00:13:24.660
So you can see these are the
sources that we're currently

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:26.130
monitoring.

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:28.630
To us, a source just
means could be a database.

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:32.530
It could be a VMware
instance, vSphere instance.

00:13:32.530 --> 00:13:36.830
It could be a physical server.

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:39.790
And we can see all of the
sources that we have available.

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:42.720
So we have public cloud vendors.

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:46.200
We have physical
compute so things

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:52.870
like Dell servers,
VMware, vCenter instances,

00:13:52.870 --> 00:13:55.320
the databases, which we're
going to talk about more

00:13:55.320 --> 00:13:58.440
today, things like load
balancers-- popular load

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:03.630
balancers as well as network
switches, physical storage.

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:07.780
So your NetApp or your Nimble
or EMC is all available.

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:11.910
And even some manager of
managers or larger applications

00:14:11.910 --> 00:14:15.630
like SAP, IAS, Jboss.

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:18.600
So it's pretty wide ranging
and it's rapidly expanding.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:20.940
Like Marie said,
we're always looking

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:22.560
for feedback from
customers to hear

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:28.440
what you're looking for next, so
we can add that to our roadmap.

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:30.540
But today I have a
Postgres database

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:34.680
that's running on my laptop,
so I'm going to choose that.

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:38.170
I will choose the new
collector that I just created.

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:39.128
And we'll configure it.

00:14:39.128 --> 00:14:41.337
MARIE COSGROVE-DAVIES: And
as Mike is configuring it,

00:14:41.337 --> 00:14:43.920
we're going to ask all of you
to not hack his local Postgres

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:46.410
database using the credentials
he's about to type in.

00:14:53.630 --> 00:14:54.600
It's extremely secure.

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:55.860
MICHEAL KELLY: Yeah.

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:57.360
And no SSL.

00:14:57.360 --> 00:14:59.040
That's how I secure my database.

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:00.520
So we'll test the connection.

00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:04.570
And what this is going to do--

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:06.940
so if I had put in the
incorrect credentials--

00:15:06.940 --> 00:15:08.040
I'll even show you.

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:09.450
Let's say this is wrong.

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:10.980
If I hit test
connection, this is

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:14.047
going to attempt to log in,
attempt to collect data,

00:15:14.047 --> 00:15:15.880
and then identify if
there are any problems.

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:17.790
So we want to have a
good tight feedback

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:21.790
loop to make this configuration
process as easy as possible,

00:15:21.790 --> 00:15:24.270
and this is one of the areas
I think can be challenging.

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:26.020
If you're dealing with
a large environment

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:27.702
and you need to get
monitoring setup,

00:15:27.702 --> 00:15:29.160
if you don't have
that feedback, it

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:31.140
can take a lot longer
than necessary.

00:15:31.140 --> 00:15:32.020
It's really simple.

00:15:32.020 --> 00:15:33.437
This is pretty
much what you would

00:15:33.437 --> 00:15:35.730
need in the vast
majority of databases

00:15:35.730 --> 00:15:37.650
that you want to
monitor, just to host

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:41.850
a port that will provide
the default and credentials.

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:45.450
And we'll let you know if you
click through into our help

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:48.240
settings, we'll show the
minimum user required

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:50.040
for every database type.

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:53.160
So we're not asking you to give
up admin rights to a database

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:54.000
to monitor it.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:55.890
We will have the
minimum required

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:58.970
user for each of these.

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:02.670
Going to click Add and
this will now show up

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:04.057
inside of my environment.

00:16:04.057 --> 00:16:05.640
MARIE COSGROVE-DAVIES:
And, Mike, what

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:07.557
if I had a bunch of
environments that I wanted

00:16:07.557 --> 00:16:08.910
to install the collector in?

00:16:08.910 --> 00:16:10.630
MICHEAL KELLY: That
is a good question.

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:15.610
So for customers that have large
environments or a lot of these,

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:17.740
we're not going to ask you
to go in through the API

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:19.740
or through the UI
every single time.

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:21.330
We have an API
designed specifically

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:23.640
for that to allow
you to script out

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:27.638
the installation of collectors,
even attach to a chef

00:16:27.638 --> 00:16:28.680
script, things like that.

00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:34.940
So now you can see, I
have a Postgres database.

00:16:34.940 --> 00:16:36.610
It's collecting data.

00:16:36.610 --> 00:16:40.575
It's sending all of that
data directly to Stackdriver.

00:16:40.575 --> 00:16:43.800
And if I drill in, probably
have all the metrics

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:44.670
shut off right now.

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:48.720
But just to give you an
idea of the type of things

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:53.100
that we gather, these are all
of the available resource types.

00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:57.280
So you can see we have obviously
the databases and instances

00:16:57.280 --> 00:17:01.530
but also things like the
index, the query, query plans,

00:17:01.530 --> 00:17:02.040
and more.

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:06.329
And if I drill into one
of these specifically,

00:17:06.329 --> 00:17:11.430
you can see all of the specific
metrics that are available.

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:16.480
Now one of the things we do
because we're really intended--

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:20.490
we're intending to serve
a wide audience here,

00:17:20.490 --> 00:17:22.736
and so we don't
expect you necessarily

00:17:22.736 --> 00:17:23.819
need all of these metrics.

00:17:23.819 --> 00:17:27.480
And we'll let you
know what the KPIs are

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:30.068
and what may make the most
sense by flagging them here.

00:17:30.068 --> 00:17:31.860
So if you're setting
this up and you're not

00:17:31.860 --> 00:17:33.720
really sure what
you want to look at,

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:37.170
we recommend that you just
enable the KPIs to get started.

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:39.780
And for the folks that if it's
your primary responsibility

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:41.530
to manage databases,
there's a good chance

00:17:41.530 --> 00:17:43.140
you know what you
want to look at.

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:46.073
You have ideas of what
those specific metrics are,

00:17:46.073 --> 00:17:47.990
and you can go through
pick those out as well.

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:54.070
So that's an overview
of BindPlane,

00:17:54.070 --> 00:17:57.010
but now I want to show you
what it looks like we actually

00:17:57.010 --> 00:17:58.330
jump into Stackdriver.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:05.670
So this is Stackdriver.

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:10.103
This is the project that I'm
sending all that data to.

00:18:10.103 --> 00:18:11.520
The first thing I
want to show you

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:15.480
is just the access we have now.

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:19.365
So just like with your Google
metrics, click in here.

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:32.330
I just want to filter and
show you that we have--

00:18:32.330 --> 00:18:34.730
so if I want to do a search
on Cassandra metrics,

00:18:34.730 --> 00:18:37.655
those are all available here.

00:18:37.655 --> 00:18:39.280
Let's say I want to
look for some Cisco

00:18:39.280 --> 00:18:45.000
hardware, a little bit
of a slow search but--

00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:52.640
MARIE COSGROVE-DAVIES: Turns
out the Wi-Fi in movie theaters

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:53.598
is not always the best.

00:18:56.656 --> 00:18:58.672
MICHEAL KELLY: One
more shot here.

00:18:58.672 --> 00:18:59.835
There we go.

00:18:59.835 --> 00:19:02.120
So now you see all
the Cisco so this

00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:05.820
would be your Cisco hardware
servers also network here.

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:06.750
It's all available.

00:19:06.750 --> 00:19:10.050
Everything that you saw
there that we're sending over

00:19:10.050 --> 00:19:13.025
is now-- now shows up inside
of your metric explore.

00:19:13.025 --> 00:19:14.400
And you can use
it for everything

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:17.070
that you're normally using
Stackdriver for so you

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:19.710
can create alerts on it.

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:22.800
We have some that
are available here.

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:25.680
So here's an example of
an alert that we've set up

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:28.080
for a database specifically.

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:31.540
So it's a buffer
cache hit ratio check.

00:19:31.540 --> 00:19:34.860
So we have this on all of
our SQL Server databases

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:38.580
to flag when we notice that
there is a buffer cache hit

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:40.290
significantly below well--

00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:44.250
below 1 for over a
certain amount of time.

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:47.130
And that's an important one
for us to keep an eye on.

00:19:47.130 --> 00:19:50.072
And what we've done is
just in case somebody that

00:19:50.072 --> 00:19:52.530
may not be familiar with this
comes in and gets this alert,

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:54.270
we're going to make sure
that it's really clear what

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:55.130
they need to do.

00:19:55.130 --> 00:19:56.630
So we've provided
the documentation,

00:19:56.630 --> 00:19:59.940
the links to where you can
go to get more details on how

00:19:59.940 --> 00:20:00.720
to resolve that.

00:20:02.982 --> 00:20:04.440
And what we're
doing today is we've

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:08.710
built up a list of these
incidents that are available.

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:12.480
We can provide them to you
as part of the installation

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:15.960
process if that's helpful.

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:18.800
Or for folks that have a really
good idea of what they want

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:21.450
and what they need to see,
it's easy to build your own

00:20:21.450 --> 00:20:25.560
and build your own
alert structure.

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:30.042
So next thing I want to take
a look at was the dashboards.

00:20:30.042 --> 00:20:31.500
And this is just
giving you an idea

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:35.800
of what you can do with
Stackdriver and with all

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:38.650
the information
that's available.

00:20:38.650 --> 00:20:42.630
So in this case, what I have
is one of those databases

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:44.480
that was running on prem.

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:48.300
It's running on a
Pure Storage array.

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:49.410
It's using VMware.

00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:52.090
It's running in vCenter as well.

00:20:52.090 --> 00:20:55.173
And I have an issue that
came in from a customer.

00:20:55.173 --> 00:20:57.090
So the application that's
using this database,

00:20:57.090 --> 00:21:00.690
they've said that
they've noticed

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:02.335
deteriorating conditions.

00:21:02.335 --> 00:21:03.960
And so we want to go
in and take a look

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:05.880
at how do we find
that-- how do we

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:07.655
find where that's coming from.

00:21:07.655 --> 00:21:09.030
And this is one
of the big things

00:21:09.030 --> 00:21:13.770
that we're trying to solve
here is how do we prevent teams

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:16.800
from pointing the finger
at the other group.

00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:19.833
So is it a database
issue if you're on prem?

00:21:19.833 --> 00:21:21.000
There's a lot of complexity.

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:22.810
Is it actually a
storage issue, or is it

00:21:22.810 --> 00:21:24.780
a virtualization issue?

00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:27.310
And that's what we're
identifying here.

00:21:27.310 --> 00:21:29.790
So if you look what we've
done is pull together

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:33.190
some key metrics from all of
the components in the stack.

00:21:33.190 --> 00:21:37.800
So I'm looking at the Pure
Storage host latency, which

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:44.550
is spiky, but nothing is jumping
out at me as a clear issue.

00:21:44.550 --> 00:21:45.780
I can go and I see--

00:21:45.780 --> 00:21:49.540
well, I'm seeing some repetitive
vCenter host memory used.

00:21:49.540 --> 00:21:52.880
So these are spikes
in the host memory

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:56.670
that that SQL Server
database is running on.

00:21:56.670 --> 00:21:58.500
I also see disk weight,
and I can drill--

00:21:58.500 --> 00:22:01.240
I can identify the specific
database this is running on.

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:05.082
So it's not disk wait
on tempDB or master.

00:22:05.082 --> 00:22:08.440
This is specifically on
my app zero database.

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:10.050
So I know there's wait time.

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:12.870
Operations are
increasing as well.

00:22:12.870 --> 00:22:15.570
I'm seeing a large spike
in activation count,

00:22:15.570 --> 00:22:18.330
and here's the problem.

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:19.920
So I see specifically--

00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:22.470
I can narrow it
down to the queries,

00:22:22.470 --> 00:22:24.420
and I can get access to
that individual query,

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:28.470
the query text, that seems
to be causing a major spike

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:30.240
at this time every hour.

00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:31.740
So what I'm going
to do is go back--

00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:32.730
if I'm the
application owner, I'm

00:22:32.730 --> 00:22:34.813
going to take a look at
what's running this query.

00:22:34.813 --> 00:22:38.400
Either optimize it, or
we'll modify the frequency

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:39.690
that that's running.

00:22:39.690 --> 00:22:43.140
But it lets you get
through what could normally

00:22:43.140 --> 00:22:46.200
take a lot of
interactions jumping back

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:49.035
and forth between a storage
team, virtualization

00:22:49.035 --> 00:22:54.700
team, a database team,
or the Cloud team.

00:22:54.700 --> 00:22:59.200
So that's one example
of an on prem use case.

00:22:59.200 --> 00:23:03.193
Let's take a look at
something similar in--

00:23:03.193 --> 00:23:04.360
if you're running it in GCP.

00:23:08.130 --> 00:23:12.060
So really what we're trying
to show here is side by side

00:23:12.060 --> 00:23:15.600
how can we use
this data in a way

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:19.290
to help explain what's going on
and make it really obvious when

00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:23.220
an issue at the infrastructure
or GCP layer may be occurring--

00:23:23.220 --> 00:23:26.740
may be caused by the
database or vice versa.

00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:28.900
And so what we have
on the left here,

00:23:28.900 --> 00:23:30.600
these are metrics
coming from GCP.

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:33.240
So this is the
performance of the disk

00:23:33.240 --> 00:23:35.950
that a database is running on.

00:23:35.950 --> 00:23:41.040
And one thing I'll point out
is we see these read spikes.

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:43.680
And so without any
database visibility,

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:46.320
I really don't know what's
causing that read spike.

00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:53.010
But since we do have those
metrics for the SQL Server,

00:23:53.010 --> 00:23:55.560
I can take a look
at that read spike

00:23:55.560 --> 00:23:57.240
that I'm getting from GCP.

00:23:57.240 --> 00:24:00.510
I can correlate it back
to the database that's

00:24:00.510 --> 00:24:02.260
running on that.

00:24:02.260 --> 00:24:08.190
And, again, I can see it's my
app GCP database specifically

00:24:08.190 --> 00:24:11.760
that seems to be
causing that problem.

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:14.310
And I can go through-- active
connections don't seem to be--

00:24:14.310 --> 00:24:17.050
they seem to be spiking a bit.

00:24:17.050 --> 00:24:21.930
And, again, if there's a query
specifically related to that,

00:24:21.930 --> 00:24:23.790
it's easy-- we make it
really easy to find.

00:24:27.152 --> 00:24:28.610
So those are a
couple of dashboards

00:24:28.610 --> 00:24:32.750
for if you have a single
mission critical database

00:24:32.750 --> 00:24:37.522
or something that you want
to really focus in on.

00:24:37.522 --> 00:24:40.400
But what about if you have
just a very large number

00:24:40.400 --> 00:24:42.390
of databases.

00:24:42.390 --> 00:24:45.590
So if you have several dozen
that you're trying to manage,

00:24:45.590 --> 00:24:47.780
maybe they're all the same type.

00:24:47.780 --> 00:24:49.430
Maybe there's a variety.

00:24:49.430 --> 00:24:52.080
And really what you're
looking for are outliers.

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:55.640
So if you want to find something
that maybe is experiencing

00:24:55.640 --> 00:25:02.240
issues inside of a, say, in
this case of SQL Server farm.

00:25:02.240 --> 00:25:03.930
And this is really
easy to do as well.

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:06.830
So what we have in
this dashboard is

00:25:06.830 --> 00:25:10.695
we're monitoring
three dozen databases.

00:25:10.695 --> 00:25:12.320
And we have some of
the key performance

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:14.430
indicators for all of those.

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:17.000
So we can identify when one
of them is outside of the norm

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:20.300
whether it's things
like disk wait time--

00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:26.120
can see the cache ratio, the
CPU usage, queries instant lock

00:25:26.120 --> 00:25:27.663
wait time.

00:25:27.663 --> 00:25:29.580
And so a couple of things
I'll see right away,

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:32.300
number 1 is that we see
some of these spikes

